          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              “This disappearance and regeneration of space in time and of time in space is motion;– a becoming, which, however, is itself just as much immediately the identically existing unity of both, or matter.” &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#PN261"}> PdN§261
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The term
            %em geometrodynamics
            has been coined, or at least promoted, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Wheeler"} John Wheeler
            as a description for the dynamics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            according to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is entirely encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} metric geometry
          %p
            More specifically the term
            %em geometrodynamics
            is associated with Wheeler’s speculation that all of physics might fundamentally be described by configurations of gravity coupled to other fields, notably the field of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            without any matter content but with certain nontrivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            may locally effectively look as if they contained massive and possibly charged bodies.
          %p
            For instance on a spacetime that is obtained from two copies of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            connected by a thin (as measured by the metric) throat – often called a
            %em wormhole
            – an electric field configuration whose field lines all converge to the throat’s mouth in one of the two Minkowski sheets, pass through the throat and then emerge concentrically in the other Minkowski sheet may have no divergence anywhere, hence according to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} Maxwell's equations
            have no charge sources anywhere, and still effectively look to an observer constrained to one of the two Minkowski sheets but relatively far away from the throat’s mouth as if they were the field lines of a positively or negatively charged point source located where the mouth of the throat is.
          %p
            These kinds of ideas Wheeler liked to describe by phrases such as
            %em charge without charge
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em mass without mass
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity+as+a+BF-theory"} gravity as a BF-theory
          %h3#similarity_to_kkcompactifications_of_pure_supergravity Similarity to KK-compactifications of pure super-gravity
          %p
            A rather similar perspective arises in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            of systems of pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theories"} effective field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            these reductions give rise to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theories"} effective field theories
            which contain not just extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            field (such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            in the original KK-mechanism) but also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G2-MSSM"} G2-MSSM
            consist entirely of pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+supergravity"} 11d supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactified
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G2-manifold"} G2-manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            and the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            contains all of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
